PG.notes("l329", "<p class=\"summary\">Short project intro for the DBANK DeFi app: a simplified money-market idea in which a token balance earns interest, written in Motoko for the Internet Computer. Also positions Motoko as a general-purpose language that feels familiar to JS/TS/Swift/Java/C# developers.</p>\n<h3>The inspiration: money-market protocols</h3>\n<ul><li>Compound is a money-market protocol: holders supply tokens (e.g. Ether) to earn interest, and borrowers take out tokens such as Chainlink and repay later.</li><li>Borrowers pay interest; the protocol passes it to lenders and keeps a small fee for running the service.</li><li>DBANK is a cut-down version: no borrowing side, just one account whose balance grows with interest over time.</li></ul>\n<h3>What the project will cover</h3>\n<ul><li>A Motoko backend (canister) that stores a balance, accepts top-ups and withdrawals, and compounds interest based on elapsed time.</li><li>A JavaScript/HTML/CSS frontend that talks to that backend (built in later lessons of this section).</li><li>Final goal for the backend: earn interest on a token balance, implemented purely in Motoko.</li></ul>\n<h3>About Motoko</h3>\n<ul><li>Motoko comes from DFINITY, with Andreas Rossberg (a WebAssembly co-creator) as lead designer.</li><li>It is designed as a modern general-purpose language, aimed specifically at writing Internet Computer smart contracts (canisters).</li><li>Syntax resembles JavaScript, TypeScript, Swift, C# and Java, so prior experience transfers quickly.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Motoko compiles to WebAssembly, which is what canisters run as on the Internet Computer.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l330", "<p class=\"summary\">Creates the DBANK project with the dfx SDK, then builds a first Motoko actor from scratch: a mutable balance variable, reassignment with :=, an immutable let constant, importing the Debug module, and printing values with debug_show. Ends with a small challenge to print the constant.</p>\n<h3>Creating the project</h3>\n<ul><li>Work from the folder set up earlier for IC projects (cd into it), then run <code>dfx new dbank</code>. The SDK scaffolds the whole project; it takes roughly 2-5 minutes depending on the machine.</li><li>Open the new folder in the code editor. Notable items: README, a git repo, node_modules/config files (ignore these), and src, where all code goes.</li><li>The backend file is a main.mo (Motoko) with starter code. Delete the contents so everything is written from scratch.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo was generated with a newer dfx template, so the backend folder is src/DBANK_backend/main.mo and a frontend canister sits beside it; dfx.json lists both canisters.</li></ul>\n<h3>The actor (canister) skeleton</h3>\n<ul><li>In Motoko, the unit that becomes a canister is an <code>actor</code>. Write the actor keyword, a name (here DBank), and a block in braces. That alone defines a canister.</li><li>Inside the actor most ordinary programming ideas apply, and every statement ends with a semicolon.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference code writes <code>persistent actor DBank { ... }</code>; newer Motoko versions use the persistent keyword so state survives upgrades by default.</li></ul>\n<h3>Variables, naming and reassignment</h3>\n<ul><li><code>var currentValue = 300;</code> declares a mutable variable holding the bank balance, starting at 300.</li><li>Naming convention from the Motoko style guide: lowerCamelCase for variables, constants and anything dynamic; UpperCamelCase for actors, modules and types. The guide also recommends two-space indentation. These are style conventions and don't change how the code runs.</li><li>Reassignment uses <code>:=</code>, not <code>=</code>. <code>currentValue := 100;</code> replaces the stored value, so after that line the variable holds 100. A single <code>=</code> is only for the initial declaration.</li></ul>\n<h3>Debugging output with Debug.print</h3>\n<ul><li>The Debug module lives in the Motoko base library. Import with <code>import Debug &quot;mo:base/Debug&quot;;</code> where <code>mo:</code> marks a Motoko package, <code>base</code> is the base library, then the module name.</li><li><code>Debug.print(&quot;Hello&quot;)</code> writes text to the terminal that runs the local replica, similar to console.log.</li><li>print only accepts Text. Passing the number variable gives a type mismatch error because currentValue is a Nat (natural number: whole, non-negative).</li><li>Wrap non-text values in <code>debug_show(...)</code> to convert them to text for printing: <code>Debug.print(debug_show(currentValue))</code>. Large numbers print with underscore thousands separators.</li></ul>\n<h3>Running it locally</h3>\n<ul><li>Terminal 1: <code>dfx start</code> launches the local Internet Computer simulation (served on localhost, port 8000 in the lecture) and must stay running.</li><li>Terminal 2, inside the project folder: <code>dfx deploy</code> creates and installs the canister. Prints from the actor appear in terminal 1 at deploy time.</li><li>Edit, save, run <code>dfx deploy</code> again to see updated output.</li></ul>\n<h3>let for constants</h3>\n<ul><li><code>let id = 3244896453846;</code> declares an immutable binding, like const in JavaScript. Use it for data that must never change.</li><li>Trying <code>id := ...</code> fails at compile time because it is immutable.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Project setup commands</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">cd ic-projects\ndfx new dbank\ncd dbank\n\n# terminal 1\ndfx start\n# terminal 2 (inside the project)\ndfx deploy</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">First DBank actor (matches the reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Debug &quot;mo:base/Debug&quot;;\n\npersistent actor DBank {\n  var currentValue = 300;\n  currentValue := 100;\n\n  let id = 3244896453846;\n\n  Debug.print(&quot;Hello&quot;);\n  Debug.print(debug_show(currentValue));\n  Debug.print(debug_show(id));\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Print the constant id</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The actor from this lesson with currentValue, the let id constant and a Debug.print(&quot;Hello&quot;) line, deployed locally.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Make the id constant show up in the terminal output.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Pause and make sure the lesson code is written locally.</li><li>Call Debug.print with debug_show(id) as the argument (plain id would be a type error).</li><li>Save and run dfx deploy in the second terminal; check the first terminal for the number.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The terminal prints the large id number with underscore separators.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">Debug.print(debug_show(id));</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Passing a Nat directly to Debug.print causes a type mismatch; use debug_show.</li><li>Using = instead of := when reassigning.</li><li>Trying to reassign a let constant.</li><li>Running dfx deploy in the same terminal as dfx start (stops the replica); use a second terminal.</li><li>Forgetting the semicolon at the end of statements.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l331", "<p class=\"summary\">Adds functions to the DBank actor: private then public, with a typed Nat parameter. Shows calling canister methods through dfx canister call and through the generated Candid UI, and ends with a challenge to write a withdraw function.</p>\n<h3>Defining functions with func</h3>\n<ul><li>Functions use the <code>func</code> keyword (Swift-like), then a name, parameter list in parentheses and a body in braces. First version: topUp with no parameters that adds 1 to currentValue.</li><li>Finish the function with a print of the new value (Debug.print with debug_show).</li><li>The closing brace of a function declaration needs a semicolon after it; leaving it out causes errors. Motoko expects a semicolon after every declaration.</li><li>Calling it inside the actor body (<code>topUp();</code>) runs it at deploy time, so the printed value is one higher (101 in the demo).</li></ul>\n<h3>Private vs public functions</h3>\n<ul><li>A plain func is private: it can only be used inside the actor's braces.</li><li>Trying <code>dfx canister call dbank topUp</code> against a private function errors with a message saying the canister has no update method with that name.</li><li>Add <code>public</code> before func to expose it to the outside world (command line, other canisters, a frontend). Redeploy with dfx deploy after editing.</li></ul>\n<h3>Calling canister methods from the CLI</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: <code>dfx canister call &lt;canister-name&gt; &lt;function-name&gt; [argument]</code>. The canister name comes from dfx.json / the project (lowercase dbank in the lecture).</li><li>Arguments go in parentheses, in Candid syntax, e.g. <code>(12)</code>; with no inputs nothing is needed.</li></ul>\n<h3>Candid UI</h3>\n<ul><li>Candid is an interface description language: it describes a canister's public methods and types, independent of the language it was written in (Motoko, Rust). Candid UI reads that description and builds a clickable test page, comparable to the try-it-out section of interactive API docs.</li><li>Setup: get the Candid UI canister ID with <code>dfx canister id __Candid_UI</code> (two leading underscores). Open the local replica address with <code>?canisterId=&lt;that id&gt;</code> in the query string.</li><li>The page then asks for the ID of your own canister: get it with <code>dfx canister id dbank</code> and paste it in. A .did file is not needed, so ignore that prompt. Press Go.</li><li>The UI lists each public function with a Call button and input fields. Results/prints still appear in the terminal running dfx start.</li><li>After changing the code and redeploying, refresh the Candid UI page to see new or changed functions.</li></ul>\n<h3>Parameters and the Nat type</h3>\n<ul><li>Add a parameter inside the parentheses as <code>name : Type</code>. Here <code>amount : Nat</code>.</li><li>Nat is a natural number: zero and above, no negatives or decimals. Candid UI shows a numeric field and states that it expects a natural number.</li><li>Replace the fixed +1 with <code>currentValue += amount;</code>. <code>+=</code> and <code>-=</code> are compound-assign operators like JavaScript.</li></ul>\n<h3>State resets on redeploy</h3>\n<ul><li>After redeploying, the balance went back to its initial value (100), so a top-up of 12 gave 112 rather than 103+12. Each dfx deploy re-initialised the variables in this version.</li><li>The lecture defers the explanation to the orthogonal persistence lesson.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">topUp and withdraw as public functions with a Nat parameter</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">persistent actor DBank {\n  var currentValue = 300;\n  currentValue := 100;\n\n  public func topUP(amount : Nat) {\n    currentValue += amount;\n    Debug.print(debug_show (currentValue));\n  };\n\n  public func withdraw(amount : Nat) {\n    currentValue -= amount;\n    Debug.print(debug_show (currentValue));\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">CLI and Candid UI helper commands</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">dfx deploy\ndfx canister call dbank topUP '(12)'\ndfx canister id __Candid_UI\ndfx canister id dbank\n# open: http://localhost:8000/?canisterId=&lt;candid_ui_id&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Write a withdraw function</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The DBank actor with a public topUp(amount : Nat) function and Candid UI working.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Add a public function that subtracts a given amount from currentValue and prints the result.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Declare <code>public func withdraw(amount : Nat)</code> (a public function so Candid UI and the CLI can reach it).</li><li>Inside, use <code>currentValue -= amount;</code>.</li><li>Print the new value with Debug.print(debug_show(currentValue)); end the function with a semicolon.</li><li>Run dfx deploy, refresh Candid UI, call the function with an amount such as 10 and check the terminal shows the lower balance.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Withdrawing 10 from 100 prints 90. Withdrawing more than the balance traps with a natural-subtraction underflow error, which the next lesson fixes with conditionals.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public func withdraw(amount : Nat) {\n  currentValue -= amount;\n  Debug.print(debug_show (currentValue));\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the semicolon after a function's closing brace.</li><li>Calling a function that is not public: the CLI says there is no such update method.</li><li>Forgetting to redeploy and refresh Candid UI after edits.</li><li>Mixing up the Candid UI canister id and your own canister id.</li><li>Assuming the balance survives a redeploy (it does not yet).</li><li>Withdrawing more than the balance: Nat underflow error.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo spells the function topUP, so CLI calls must use that exact casing.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l332", "<p class=\"summary\">Fixes the Nat underflow bug in withdraw using an if/else, and explains why an explicit Int type annotation on the subtraction result is needed. Shows that Motoko control flow looks like JavaScript.</p>\n<h3>The bug being fixed</h3>\n<ul><li>currentValue is a Nat, so it cannot go below zero. Withdrawing 999 from a balance of 100 raises a 'Natural subtraction underflow' error and the call fails.</li><li>The fix is a guard: only subtract when the result would stay at or above zero.</li></ul>\n<h3>if / else syntax</h3>\n<ul><li>Conditionals resemble JavaScript: <code>if (condition) { ... } else { ... }</code>. Comparison operators such as &gt;= work as expected.</li><li>Inside the if branch: subtract the amount from currentValue and print the new balance.</li><li>The else branch prints a message for the developer instead of changing anything. Because that message is plain Text, it needs no debug_show.</li><li>Statement blocks end with semicolons, including after the closing brace of the whole if/else.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why a type annotation is needed</h3>\n<ul><li>Writing <code>(currentValue - amount) &gt;= 0</code> directly produces a yellow warning that the operator may trap or that the inferred type is Nat. A subtraction of two Nats can't be negative in the type system, so the comparison to 0 is not meaningful as typed.</li><li>Fix: state the type of the result. It can be done in a separate constant: <code>let tempValue : Int = currentValue - amount;</code> where Int is any whole number, positive or negative. Then compare tempValue &gt;= 0.</li><li>The syntax for an annotation is <code>name : Type</code>, the same style as function parameters (amount : Nat).</li><li>The reference solution also shows the inline form: <code>((currentValue - amount) : Int &gt;= 0)</code>, annotating inside the condition without a temp constant.</li><li>Types so far: amount is Nat, currentValue is Nat (inferred from a positive literal, though it can be spelled out), tempValue is Int. A positive integer literal defaults to Nat.</li></ul>\n<h3>Result</h3>\n<ul><li>A large withdrawal no longer crashes: the call succeeds, the balance is untouched, and the else message prints.</li><li>A reasonable withdrawal (e.g. 10 from 100) subtracts normally.</li><li>Takeaway: most language features carry over from other languages; the type system and canister interactions are what differ.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">withdraw with an if/else guard and Int annotation (reference-style)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Debug &quot;mo:base/Debug&quot;;\nimport Int &quot;mo:base/Int&quot;;\n\npublic func withdraw(amount : Nat) {\n  let tempValue : Int = currentValue - amount;   // explicit type for the result\n\n  if (tempValue &gt;= 0) {\n    currentValue -= amount;\n    Debug.print(debug_show (currentValue));\n  } else {\n    Debug.print(&quot;Amount too large, current value less than zero.&quot;);\n  };\n};</pre>\n<h2>The task: Add an else branch for oversized withdrawals</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> withdraw subtracts the amount with a guard on tempValue but has no else branch.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Print a helpful message when the requested amount exceeds the balance.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add an else block to the if statement.</li><li>Inside it call Debug.print with a text message (no debug_show needed for text).</li><li>Redeploy, call withdraw with a large number in Candid UI, and confirm the message appears and the balance is unchanged.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Large withdrawal: message printed, no error. Normal withdrawal: balance reduced.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">} else {\n  Debug.print(&quot;Amount too large, current value less than zero.&quot;);\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Comparing a Nat subtraction to 0 without annotating the result type.</li><li>Using debug_show on plain text (unneeded) or forgetting it on numbers.</li><li>Missing semicolon after the if/else block.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's message contains a typo ('currnet'); fix when copying.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l333", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains why calls that change canister state take seconds (update calls go through consensus) while read-only query calls return in milliseconds. Adds a public query function checkBalance with an async return type.</p>\n<h3>Update vs query calls</h3>\n<ul><li>topUp and withdraw modify a variable, so they are update methods: the change must be written to the blockchain and agreed by the network, which takes around 2-3 seconds in the demo (2.14 s measured).</li><li>A query method only reads state and changes nothing, so it skips consensus and answers in milliseconds (0.014 s measured in Candid UI).</li><li>Rule of thumb: if the function does not change state, make it a query; reserve update calls for real writes. Important for responsive apps and games.</li><li>By default a public func is an update method; the query modifier opts into the fast read path.</li></ul>\n<h3>Declaring a query function</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: <code>public query func checkBalance() : async Nat { ... };</code> The query keyword goes between public and func.</li><li>Return type is declared after a colon following the parameter list.</li><li>Shared (public) functions that return a value must have an async return type, so the type is written <code>async Nat</code> rather than plain Nat; otherwise the compiler complains that a shared query function needs an async syntactic return type.</li><li>Inside the body use <code>return currentValue;</code>.</li><li>Declarations inside an actor are separated by semicolons. A missing semicolon after the previous function causes an error. The final one can technically omit it, but adding it everywhere keeps things consistent.</li></ul>\n<h3>Sync vs async (concept)</h3>\n<ul><li>Synchronous work runs one step at a time, each step blocking the next, like a single track; asynchronous work lets tasks proceed independently and finish in any order.</li><li>Web analogy: a modern page fires many requests at once and renders resources as they arrive, so slow ones don't block fast ones or user interaction.</li><li>Canister results come back asynchronously, which is why the async keyword shows up in return types.</li></ul>\n<h3>Seeing it in Candid UI</h3>\n<ul><li>After redeploy and refreshing Candid UI, checkBalance shows a Query button (update functions show Call). Clicking it returns the balance immediately with a timing readout.</li><li>Comparing this with clicking Call on topUp or withdraw shows the latency gap directly.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Read-only query method</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public query func checkBalance() : async Nat {\n  return currentValue;\n};</pre>\n<h2>The task: Add checkBalance and compare latency</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> DBank with public topUP and withdraw update functions.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Expose a fast read-only balance getter and observe the speed difference.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add <code>public query func checkBalance() : async Nat</code> returning currentValue.</li><li>Make sure the previous function ends with a semicolon.</li><li>Run dfx deploy, refresh Candid UI, press Query on checkBalance and note the time.</li><li>Press Call on topUp or withdraw and compare.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Query returns in a few hundredths of a second; update calls take roughly 2-3 seconds.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public query func checkBalance() : async Nat {\n  return currentValue;\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Omitting async in the return type of a public function.</li><li>Missing semicolon between function declarations.</li><li>Using a query method to change state (changes are not persisted).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Local replica timings are far shorter than a mainnet round trip would be for updates; treat figures as illustrative.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l334", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces orthogonal persistence: a canister can keep its variable state across redeploys without a database. A variable marked stable survives upgrades; a plain variable is reset. Demonstrated by redeploying with and without the stable keyword.</p>\n<h3>What persistence means here</h3>\n<ul><li>Normally, restarting or redeploying a program resets variables to their initial values; the earlier increments are lost. A JS demo makes this obvious: add 5 to a variable, remove that line, reload, and the value is back to the start.</li><li>To keep data across runs in ordinary software you need explicit persistence: a database, with code to write and read it.</li><li>On the Internet Computer a canister can retain its whole state (variables, collections, objects) automatically. It is called orthogonal because the developer does not have to write any storage code for it; it comes from the platform design.</li></ul>\n<h3>stable vs flexible variables</h3>\n<ul><li>A normal <code>var</code> is a flexible variable and is reset on upgrade. Flexible is the default, matching other languages.</li><li>Prefix with <code>stable</code> to opt in: <code>stable var currentValue : Nat = 300;</code> Now the value survives redeploys and restarts, even downtime.</li><li>A stable declaration still has an initial value, used only the first time the canister is installed.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo notes that in newer Motoko with <code>persistent actor</code>, variables are stable implicitly, so the explicit keyword triggers a redundant-stable warning (M0218). Type annotation <code>: Nat</code> is added in that code.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why the reassignment line must go</h3>\n<ul><li>A statement like <code>currentValue := 100;</code> in the actor body runs every time the actor code is initialised (each deploy), overwriting whatever was restored.</li><li>To see persistence working, comment out that line. The declaration's initial value applies once; the replace operator would clobber it every time.</li></ul>\n<h3>Experiment to run</h3>\n<ul><li>Without stable: deploy, query (300), topUp 100, query (400); change any line (e.g. add a Debug.print) and redeploy; query shows 300 again.</li><li>With stable: repeat the same steps; after redeploy the query still reports 400.</li><li>Use checkBalance (query) to read the value, and Candid UI to call topUp.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Stable variable (reference repo version, reassignment commented out)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">persistent actor DBank {\n  stable var currentValue : Nat = 300;\n  // currentValue := 100;   // would overwrite the persisted value on every deploy\n\n  public func topUP(amount : Nat) {\n    currentValue += amount;\n  };\n\n  public query func checkBalance() : async Nat {\n    return currentValue;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<h2>The task: Observe orthogonal persistence</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> DBank with topUp and checkBalance, currentValue starting at 300.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> See the balance reset on redeploy without stable, and survive with stable.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Comment out the <code>currentValue := 100;</code> line.</li><li>Deploy, use Candid UI to topUp 100 and query: 400.</li><li>Make a trivial edit, redeploy, query again: back to 300.</li><li>Add the stable keyword to the variable declaration, deploy, topUp 100 (400).</li><li>Make another trivial edit, redeploy and query: still 400.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Without stable the balance resets to 300; with stable it stays at 400 after redeploys.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">stable var currentValue : Nat = 300;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Leaving the := reassignment in place, which resets the value on each run and hides the effect of stable.</li><li>Expecting non-stable vars to survive redeploys.</li><li>Forgetting that an initial value is only used on first install for stable variables.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Changing a stable variable's type between deployments can need migration code; keep types compatible while experimenting.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l335", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains the compound-interest formula, then implements a compound() function in the DBank actor. Uses the Time module for timestamps, converts nanoseconds to seconds, raises 1.01 to the elapsed seconds with **, switches balances from Nat to Float to make the types agree, and resets startTime after each compounding.</p>\n<h3>Compound interest in brief</h3>\n<ul><li>Interest earned is added to the principal, and later interest is calculated on the larger total. Example: 1000 with 50 interest becomes 1050, which then earns interest again.</li><li>General formula: final = principal * (1 + rate/n)^t, where rate is a decimal (10% = 0.1, 5% = 0.05), n is the number of compounding periods per time unit and t is the number of time units.</li><li>For the demo: rate 1% (0.01) per second, compounded once per second, so n = 1 and the multiplier is 1.01^seconds. This is an unrealistically high rate chosen so growth is visible within seconds.</li></ul>\n<h3>Tracking time with the Time module</h3>\n<ul><li>Import with <code>import Time &quot;mo:base/Time&quot;;</code>. <code>Time.now()</code> returns the current time as an integer count of nanoseconds since 1 January 1970 (Unix epoch).</li><li>Record the start: <code>let startTime = Time.now();</code> and print it with Debug.print(debug_show(startTime)) (debug_show needed because it is not Text).</li><li>Nanoseconds to seconds: divide by 1_000_000_000 (1000000000). The division on integers truncates to a whole number of seconds.</li></ul>\n<h3>The compound function</h3>\n<ul><li>Declared <code>public func</code> (not query) because it changes currentValue, so it is an update call.</li><li>Steps inside: get currentTime = Time.now(); timeElapsedNS = currentTime - startTime; timeElapsedS = timeElapsedNS / 1000000000; then update the balance.</li><li>In practice it could be called on a schedule or whenever a user tops up or withdraws; here it is triggered manually from Candid UI.</li><li>Update: <code>currentValue := currentValue * (1.01 ** Float.fromInt(timeElapsedS));</code>. The exponent operator is <code>**</code> (two asterisks). Exponents are evaluated before multiplication, but parentheses make the order explicit.</li></ul>\n<h3>Fixing type mismatches (Nat, Int, Float)</h3>\n<ul><li>1.01 is a Float but timeElapsedS is a whole number, so the exponent errors with a type mismatch. Convert it with <code>Float.fromInt(...)</code> from the base Float module (<code>import Float &quot;mo:base/Float&quot;;</code>).</li><li>currentValue was a Nat, which can't be multiplied by a Float. Converting Nat to Float is awkward in Motoko, and money should allow decimals anyway, so change the balance to <code>Float</code>: <code>stable var currentValue : Float = 300;</code>.</li><li>Then align everything else: topUP and withdraw parameters become <code>amount : Float</code>, tempValue becomes Float, and checkBalance returns <code>async Float</code>.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Time.now() returns Int, which is why Float.fromInt is the right converter.</li></ul>\n<h3>Resetting the start time</h3>\n<ul><li>If startTime stays fixed, a second compound call would count the time since the beginning again and over-pay interest, ignoring the last compounding.</li><li>Fix: after updating the balance set <code>startTime := currentTime;</code>. That requires startTime to be a var, not a let. Make it <code>stable var</code> so it persists between deploys.</li><li>After changing a stable variable's type (Nat to Float), the stored value may reset on deploy. To restore 300 and a fresh start time, temporarily keep <code>currentValue := 300;</code> and <code>startTime := Time.now();</code>, deploy once, then comment those lines out and deploy again so they don't overwrite state next time.</li></ul>\n<h3>Result</h3>\n<ul><li>Check balance starts at 300; calling compound after several seconds raises it (88 more in the demo) and each next call grows more because the principal is larger.</li><li>The prototype demonstrates a working DeFi-style interest mechanism; a real frontend comes in later lessons.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final backend for this lesson (reference repo, cleaned)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Debug &quot;mo:base/Debug&quot;;\nimport Time &quot;mo:base/Time&quot;;\nimport Float &quot;mo:base/Float&quot;;\n\npersistent actor DBank {\n  stable var currentValue : Float = 300;\n  stable var startTime = Time.now();\n\n  public func topUP(amount : Float) {\n    currentValue += amount;\n    Debug.print(debug_show (currentValue));\n  };\n\n  public func withdraw(amount : Float) {\n    let tempValue : Float = currentValue - amount;\n    if (tempValue &gt;= 0) {\n      currentValue -= amount;\n      Debug.print(debug_show (currentValue));\n    } else {\n      Debug.print(&quot;Amount too large, current value less than zero.&quot;);\n    };\n  };\n\n  public query func checkBalance() : async Float {\n    return currentValue;\n  };\n\n  public func compound() {\n    let currentTime = Time.now();\n    let timeElapsedNS = currentTime - startTime;\n    let timeElapsedS = timeElapsedNS / 1000000000;\n    currentValue := currentValue * (1.01 ** Float.fromInt(timeElapsedS));\n    startTime := currentTime;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<h2>The task: Print the current time in nanoseconds, then build compound()</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> DBank actor with topUP, withdraw, checkBalance and a stable currentValue.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Track time since the last compounding and grow the balance by 1% per elapsed second.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Import Time and Float from mo:base.</li><li>Challenge: create startTime = Time.now() and print it with debug_show; deploy and see the nanosecond count.</li><li>Change startTime to <code>stable var</code> so it can be reassigned and persists.</li><li>Add <code>public func compound()</code> with currentTime, timeElapsedNS and timeElapsedS (divide by 1000000000).</li><li>Set currentValue := currentValue * (1.01 ** Float.fromInt(timeElapsedS)).</li><li>Switch currentValue, amount parameters, tempValue and checkBalance return type from Nat to Float.</li><li>Add startTime := currentTime; at the end of compound.</li><li>Reset the balance and start time once (temporary assignments), redeploy, remove them, redeploy again.</li><li>In Candid UI check balance, wait a few seconds, call compound, check balance again.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Balance starts at 300 and grows by 1.01 to the power of elapsed seconds each time compound is called, since the last call.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public func compound() {\n  let currentTime = Time.now();\n  let timeElapsedNS = currentTime - startTime;\n  let timeElapsedS = timeElapsedNS / 1000000000;\n  currentValue := currentValue * (1.01 ** Float.fromInt(timeElapsedS));\n  startTime := currentTime;\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Mixing Nat, Int and Float in one calculation causes type errors; convert with Float.fromInt.</li><li>Leaving startTime as let: it cannot be reassigned, so interest is over-counted.</li><li>Leaving the temporary reset lines in place so every deploy overwrites persisted state.</li><li>Using a query modifier on compound (it writes state).</li><li>Forgetting the semicolon after compound's closing brace; the reference repo's version lacks one before its trailing comment, which can break compile when more declarations follow.</li><li>Expecting a 1% per-second rate to be realistic; it is only to make the demo visible.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l336", "<p class=\"summary\">With the Motoko backend finished, the default ICP template frontend is replaced by a prepared DBank page (HTML, CSS and a logo). The page is previewed locally through the dfx and npm workflow, with the JavaScript left empty until the next lecture.</p>\n<h3>Why the template frontend gets replaced</h3>\n<ul><li>A new ICP project generated from the template ships a frontend that is just the 'hello world' sample: a Dfinity logo, an index.html and a JS entry file. It knows nothing about the DBank canister's functions.</li><li>The plan is to swap in a DBank-specific UI that can show the balance, top up and withdraw, then wire it to the Motoko canister in the following lecture.</li><li>First step: empty index.html, main.css and the JS entry file (all three parts of the frontend) so they can be replaced.</li></ul>\n<h3>Supplied resources and how to use them</h3>\n<ul><li>The lesson resources contain two files with their extensions stripped (called Index and Main) so a double-click does not open them in a browser. Copy the Index text into index.html and the Main text into main.css.</li><li>A logo image (dbank_logo.png) is also supplied. Drop it into the frontend's assets folder (next to the existing logo.png). The default Dfinity logo can be deleted to avoid confusion.</li><li>The HTML and CSS are not written live because the course already taught them; the advice is to still read pasted code and make sure each part is understood.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In the reference repo the CSS lives at src/dbank_frontend/assets/main.css and is linked as /main.css, because vite.config.js sets publicDir to 'assets', so files there are served from the site root. The logo is referenced as dbank_logo.png for the same reason.</li></ul>\n<h3>Structure of the supplied page</h3>\n<ul><li>A .container div (fixed 300px wide, centred, translucent background, rounded corners) holds everything.</li><li>Inside: the logo img, an h1 'Current Balance: $' with a span id='value' holding the number, a .divider div (a short coloured bar), and a form.</li><li>The span id='value' is the element the JS will later overwrite with the live balance. The initial 234 is only placeholder text.</li><li>The form has two number inputs: id='input-amount' (name topUp) for deposits and id='withdrawal-amount' (name withdraw) for withdrawals. Both use step='0.01' and min='0' so cents are allowed and negatives are blocked by the browser.</li><li>A submit input id='submit-btn' with the label 'Finalise Transaction' sends both values in one go.</li><li>The script tag is type='module' pointing at /src/main.js, so the JS file can use import statements.</li><li>CSS styles the page with a dark background, centred text, white rounded inputs that get a blue border on :focus, hides the number spinners via the webkit spin-button pseudo-elements, and styles #submit-btn with a hover colour and a :disabled look (muted translucent). The :disabled style matters later, because the JS disables the button while the canister calls run.</li></ul>\n<h3>Running the project locally</h3>\n<ul><li>Open a terminal inside the dbank project folder and run npm install to fetch the dependencies listed in package.json (there are few).</li><li>Run dfx start to launch the simulated local Internet Computer replica. Keep this terminal open.</li><li>In a second terminal run dfx deploy to (re)build and install the canisters. Redeploying is advisable after files changed, even if it was done before.</li><li>In a third step run npm start to launch the Vite dev server and open the local site (localhost) to see the frontend.</li><li>At this stage the logo is broken until the PNG is added and nothing is interactive because the JS file is still empty. After adding the logo, save and refresh and the logo appears.</li></ul>\n<h3>Making a logo (optional)</h3>\n<ul><li>The logo used was produced with a free online logo maker: enter the product name, choose fonts, sizes, styles, a colour, optional slogan and icons, then pick from the generated options.</li><li>Downloading usually needs a free sign-up; a screenshot cropped to size works as an alternative.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Core of the supplied index.html body (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;main&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;img src=&quot;dbank_logo.png&quot; alt=&quot;DBank logo&quot; width=&quot;100&quot; /&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Current Balance: $&lt;span id=&quot;value&quot;&gt;234&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;div class=&quot;divider&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;form action=&quot;#&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;Amount to Top Up&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;input id=&quot;input-amount&quot; type=&quot;number&quot; step=&quot;0.01&quot; min=&quot;0&quot; name=&quot;topUp&quot; value=&quot;&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;Amount to Withdraw&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;input id=&quot;withdrawal-amount&quot; type=&quot;number&quot; name=&quot;withdraw&quot; step=&quot;0.01&quot; min=&quot;0&quot; value=&quot;&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;input id=&quot;submit-btn&quot; type=&quot;submit&quot; value=&quot;Finalise Transaction&quot; /&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/main&gt;\n&lt;script type=&quot;module&quot; src=&quot;/src/main.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Selected CSS: disabled button state and hidden number spinners</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">#submit-btn {\n  background-color: #617BFF;\n  border: 2px #617BFF solid;\n  color: white;\n  cursor: pointer;\n  transition: all 0.2s;\n}\n#submit-btn:hover { background-color: #8095FF; }\n#submit-btn:disabled {\n  border: 2px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1) solid;\n  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);\n}\ninput[type=number]::-webkit-inner-spin-button,\ninput[type=number]::-webkit-outer-spin-button {\n  -webkit-appearance: none;\n  margin: 0;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Startup commands</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">npm install      # install frontend dependencies\ndfx start        # terminal 1: local replica (keep running)\ndfx deploy       # terminal 2: build and install canisters\nnpm start        # run the Vite dev server for the frontend</pre>\n<h2>The task: Replace the template frontend with the DBank UI</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A dbank project with the finished Motoko backend and the default template frontend (sample HTML, CSS, JS and Dfinity logo).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> See the DBank page (logo, balance, two inputs, button) in the browser via the local dev server, ready for JS wiring.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Delete the contents of index.html, main.css and the JS entry file.</li><li>Paste the supplied Index text into index.html and the Main text into main.css.</li><li>Drag the supplied dbank_logo.png into the assets folder; optionally delete the Dfinity logo.</li><li>In the project folder run npm install.</li><li>Run dfx start in one terminal, dfx deploy in another, then npm start.</li><li>Open the local page, save and refresh to confirm the logo shows and the layout matches.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A dark card with the DBank logo, 'Current Balance' text, two number fields and a blue 'Finalise Transaction' button. Nothing happens on submit yet.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Saving the supplied files with .html/.css extensions on a double-click can open them in the browser; the stripped-extension files exist to avoid that.</li><li>Forgetting to move the logo into the assets folder leaves a broken image.</li><li>Running npm start without dfx start / dfx deploy means the canister is not available when JS is added later.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's 38.08 main.js is empty by design; code appears in 38.09.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l337", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds the JavaScript that connects the DBank page to the Motoko canister: importing the generated declarations, awaiting canister calls, handling the form submit, converting input strings to floats, guarding against empty inputs, disabling the button during slow update calls, and finally refactoring into one update function.</p>\n<h3>Importing the canister into the frontend</h3>\n<ul><li>Import the canister object with a named import from the generated declarations folder, using a relative path (up out of the frontend src folder, into declarations, then the backend canister's folder). Example name: dbank_backend (the lecture's older project calls it dbank).</li><li>The declarations folder is generated by dfx. It contains the Candid-derived interface that tells JavaScript which public functions the Motoko actor exposes and what argument types they take. It acts as the bridge between the two languages.</li><li>Once imported, canister methods are called like ordinary async functions: checkBalance(), topUp()/topUP(), withdraw(), compound().</li></ul>\n<h3>Showing the balance on page load</h3>\n<ul><li>Goal: replace the placeholder number in span#value with the real currentValue whenever the page opens. checkBalance is a query function so it answers quickly.</li><li>Register a window event listener for the page-load event. A first test with console.log (check the browser Console tab after a reload) proves the listener fires; then replace the log.</li><li>Fetch the balance into a const, grab span#value with document.getElementById and assign its innerText.</li><li>Bug seen first: the page displays '[object Promise]'. Canister calls return Promises because the answer arrives asynchronously from the blockchain. Fix: make the callback an async function and put await in front of the canister call so the code pauses until the value arrives.</li><li>The raw float has many decimals. Challenge: show only two. Solution: Math.round(value * 100) / 100 (scale up, round to an integer, scale back down).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference code uses the DOMContentLoaded event instead of load. DOMContentLoaded fires when the HTML is parsed, without waiting for images, so it triggers a bit earlier. Either works here.</li></ul>\n<h3>Handling the form submit</h3>\n<ul><li>Select the form with document.querySelector('form'). Selector rules mirror CSS: # for ids, . for classes, bare name for tags.</li><li>Attach a 'submit' listener. It fires when the submit input is clicked (or Enter pressed in a field). Make the callback async and accept the event parameter.</li><li>Test first with a console.log. It flashes and vanishes, because the form's default behaviour is to submit and reload the page. Calling event.preventDefault() at the top stops that, so fields stay filled and the page does not refresh.</li><li>Read the two inputs with document.getElementById('input-amount').value and document.getElementById('withdrawal-amount').value (names chosen: inputAmount, outputAmount, to avoid withdraw typos).</li></ul>\n<h3>Type conversion: strings to Float</h3>\n<ul><li>Calling topUp with the raw input value fails with an 'Invalid float64 argument' style error. The generated declarations state the Motoko function takes a Float (64-bit floating point), but an input's .value is a string.</li><li>Wrap values in parseFloat() to convert them to numbers before sending. The inputs already carry step='0.01' so decimals up to cents are accepted.</li><li>After conversion the call succeeds silently. The page does not refresh yet, so the balance span has to be updated again by repeating the checkBalance-and-display lines after the calls.</li><li>Test: top up by 10 and watch the displayed balance move from e.g. 1446 to 1456.</li></ul>\n<h3>Giving feedback during slow update calls</h3>\n<ul><li>Update calls (non-query) go through consensus and take roughly 2-3 seconds. With no feedback users click repeatedly, sending duplicate transactions.</li><li>Select the submit button from the event: event.target.querySelector('#submit-btn'). event.target is the form that fired submit.</li><li>Before the calls: button.setAttribute('disabled', true). After the balance refresh: button.removeAttribute('disabled').</li><li>Do not use setAttribute('disabled', false). A boolean attribute disables the element whenever it is present, regardless of value, so it must be removed instead.</li><li>The #submit-btn:disabled CSS from the previous lecture dims the button so the user sees it is locked.</li><li>After finishing, reset the field with input.value = '' so the old amount is not left behind.</li></ul>\n<h3>Guarding against empty inputs</h3>\n<ul><li>Because the single submit handler serves both fields, an empty field would still be passed on: parseFloat('') is NaN, which corrupts the stored balance (the Debug print on the backend showed nan).</li><li>Fix used in the lecture: wrap the top-up call in an if that checks the field's value string has length not equal to 0. Only then call topUp.</li><li>The withdrawal part is a student task: add a second, separate if for withdrawal-amount with the same length check, await withdraw(outputAmount), then clear that input. Two separate ifs are used (not else-if) so both a top-up and a withdrawal can happen in one submit; a plain else would be wrong.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo guards with !isNaN(parseFloat(...)) instead of checking string length. Both prevent sending NaN; the isNaN version also rejects non-numeric text.</li><li>Tip given: copy and paste ids and function names to avoid typos like withdrawl.</li></ul>\n<h3>Adding compound interest and resetting canister state</h3>\n<ul><li>Call compound() after the top-up/withdraw block so interest is applied every time the user transacts, then refresh the displayed balance.</li><li>The backend rate is intentionally huge (a fraction of a percent per elapsed second), so the balance grows visibly quickly during a demo; real interest would be far smaller.</li><li>Bad input corrupted the stored state, so the backend was reset. The stable variables only reinitialise reliably if the code actually changes between deploys. Procedure: comment out the lines that set currentValue and startTime, run dfx deploy, then uncomment them (currentValue back to 300) and deploy again. A Debug.print confirms the value.</li><li>To restart cleanly: Ctrl+C the npm and dfx processes, then run dfx start, dfx deploy, npm start again.</li></ul>\n<h3>Refactor into a single update function</h3>\n<ul><li>The check-balance-and-display lines appear twice (on load and after a transaction). Extract them into one async function (called updateBalance in the reference, update in the lecture) and call it from both places.</li><li>The next lecture lists likely errors; skip it if everything worked.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final frontend script (reference repo, lightly tidied)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import { dbank_backend } from &quot;../../declarations/dbank_backend&quot;;\n\nasync function updateBalance() {\n  const currentAmount = await dbank_backend.checkBalance();\n  document.getElementById(&quot;value&quot;).innerText =\n    Math.round(currentAmount * 100) / 100;\n}\n\nwindow.addEventListener(&quot;DOMContentLoaded&quot;, updateBalance);\n\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;form&quot;).addEventListener(&quot;submit&quot;, async function (event) {\n  event.preventDefault();\n\n  const button = event.target.querySelector(&quot;#submit-btn&quot;);\n  const inputAmount = parseFloat(document.getElementById(&quot;input-amount&quot;).value);\n  const outputAmount = parseFloat(document.getElementById(&quot;withdrawal-amount&quot;).value);\n\n  button.setAttribute(&quot;disabled&quot;, true);\n\n  if (!isNaN(inputAmount)) {\n    await dbank_backend.topUP(inputAmount);\n  }\n  if (!isNaN(outputAmount)) {\n    await dbank_backend.withdraw(outputAmount);\n  }\n\n  await dbank_backend.compound();\n  await updateBalance();\n\n  document.getElementById(&quot;input-amount&quot;).value = &quot;&quot;;\n  document.getElementById(&quot;withdrawal-amount&quot;).value = &quot;&quot;;\n  button.removeAttribute(&quot;disabled&quot;);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Backend functions the frontend calls (reference main.mo, trimmed)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">persistent actor DBank {\n  stable var currentValue : Float = 300;\n  stable var startTime = Time.now();\n\n  public func topUP(amount : Float) { currentValue += amount; };\n\n  public func withdraw(amount : Float) {\n    if ((currentValue - amount) &gt;= 0) { currentValue -= amount; }\n    else { Debug.print(&quot;Amount too large&quot;); };\n  };\n\n  public query func checkBalance() : async Float { return currentValue; };\n\n  public func compound() {\n    let currentTime = Time.now();\n    let timeElapsedS = (currentTime - startTime) / 1000000000;\n    currentValue := currentValue * (1.0001 ** Float.fromInt(timeElapsedS));\n    startTime := currentTime;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">The lecture's length-check guard for the top-up branch</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">if (document.getElementById(&quot;input-amount&quot;).value.length != 0) {\n  await dbank.topUp(inputAmount);\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Wire the DBank frontend to the Motoko canister</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The 38.08 project: HTML, CSS and logo in place, empty JS entry file, working Motoko actor with topUp, withdraw, checkBalance and compound.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> A page that shows the live balance, tops up and withdraws via the form, compounds interest on each transaction, and locks the button while waiting.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Import the canister from ../../declarations/&lt;canister name&gt;.</li><li>On page load, await checkBalance() and write the number into span#value.</li><li>Challenge: round to two decimals with Math.round(x * 100) / 100.</li><li>Add a submit listener on the form; call event.preventDefault().</li><li>Read both inputs and convert them with parseFloat.</li><li>Disable the submit button, then call topUp when the top-up field is filled.</li><li>Challenge: add a separate if that calls withdraw when the withdrawal field is filled.</li><li>Call compound(), refresh the balance, clear both inputs, and remove the disabled attribute.</li><li>Refactor the repeated balance-refresh lines into one async function.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Typing 10 in the top-up box and submitting raises the balance by about 10 plus a little interest after a 2-3 second wait; withdrawing lowers it; empty boxes cause no NaN.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const withdrawal = document.getElementById(&quot;withdrawal-amount&quot;);\nif (withdrawal.value.length != 0) {\n  await dbank.withdraw(parseFloat(withdrawal.value));\n  withdrawal.value = &quot;&quot;;\n}\nawait dbank.compound();\nawait update();</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting await: the page shows [object Promise] instead of a number.</li><li>Sending the input's string value to a Float parameter causes an invalid argument error; use parseFloat.</li><li>Empty fields yield NaN and corrupt the canister's stored balance.</li><li>Using setAttribute('disabled', false) does not re-enable a button; remove the attribute.</li><li>Using else instead of a second if prevents correct handling of separate fields.</li><li>Stable variables may not reset after a small code change; comment out, deploy, restore, deploy again.</li><li>Typos in function names such as topUP vs topUp (the reference backend spells it topUP, so the frontend call must match whatever the actor declares).</li><li>Reference repo quirk: it keeps the author's NaN guard and capitalised topUP; the lecture's own code used a string-length check.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l338", "<p class=\"summary\">A short resource article listing where to look for help when building on the Internet Computer: official docs, the CLI reference, the Dfinity forum and its developer Discord.</p>\n<h3>Support channels listed</h3>\n<ul><li>Official developer docs for the Internet Computer (the introduction and guides).</li><li>Command-line reference for dfx, useful when a command or flag is unfamiliar.</li><li>Official Dfinity forum for questions and discussions.</li><li>Official Dfinity developer Discord for quick community help.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The lecture page itself only exposes an Open button for the external link; the link list above comes from the course's reference repo copy. The old smartcontracts.org docs address has since moved to internetcomputer.org/docs, so check current URLs.</li></ul>");
